But the required_symbols list includes \"root\" and \"s1\" in examples. So nodes with symbol \"root\" must not be removed, and nodes with symbol \"s1\" must not be removed. But also \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" That means if a node's children are retained (not removed), then it cannot be removed. So we need to process nodes bottom-up: consider deeper nodes first. For each node that is removable, not root, not required symbol, we check if removing it would still preserve all required symbols. Since required symbols are about symbols present in the tree, we need to check after tentative removal: the set of symbols remaining in the tree (considering all nodes that are not removed) must include all required_symbols. Also, we must ensure that the removal does not cause a node to become a leaf with no children? The \"node with retained children\" condition means if a node has any child that is retained (not removed), then the parent cannot be removed. So we need to process in topological order: from leaves upward.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Parse the case payload: nodes list, required_symbols list. Build a mapping from node id to node dict. Build children mapping: parent -> list of children (or just for each node, we can have children list).\n2. Determine which nodes are initially retained? We'll start with all nodes retained, then we decide removals.\n3. We need to produce removal order. The description: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" So we should process nodes in decreasing depth (deepest first). For nodes at same depth, use node ID as tie break (stable).\n4. For each node in that order:\n   - If node is root (parent null) or node's symbol is in required_symbols, skip removal.\n   - If node is not removable (removable == false), skip.\n   - Check if the node has any children that are still retained (i.e., not removed). If it has any retained children, skip removal.\n   - Check if removing this node would cause any required symbol to no longer be present in the tree. To check, we need to know which symbols are present among all retained nodes after removal. We can compute the set of symbols from all nodes that are currently retained (not removed). Then if we remove this node, we remove its symbol from the set. If any required symbol is missing in the resulting set, skip removal. Also note: if this node's symbol appears in other nodes (multiple nodes with same symbol), then removal might be okay if other nodes still provide that symbol. So we need to count occurrences of each symbol among retained nodes. So we need a multi-set or count.\n   - If all checks pass, we mark this node as removed, add its id to removed_node_ids (in the order we process), and update the symbol counts.\n\n5. After processing all nodes in that order, the retained nodes are those not removed. Their IDs should be sorted (presumably by id string). The removal order is the order they were removed (the order we processed and removed).\n\nWe must ensure we do not mutate the input case object; we just read from it.\n\nAlso, the description says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That is what we do.\n\nPotential nuance: What about nodes that are not removable but are not required? They remain. No issue.\n\nWhat about nodes that become childless? That's fine, they can still be removed if they are removable and not required and root, but they might have no children. The \"node with retained children\" condition would be false if they have no children or all children removed, so they can be removed.\n\nThe removal order: We process deeper first, so removal order will be some sequence. The spec says \"actual removal order\" so we output in the order they were removed.\n\nNow, we need to output as a JSON string with `implementation` key containing the Python source code. The source code must define `solve(case)` and return a dict with `removed_node_ids` and `retained_node_ids`. The ou